# build output
dist/

# generated types
.astro/

# wrangler's local state, and the deploy-config redirect the Astro Cloudflare
# adapter used to write. That redirect pointed at dist/client/wrangler.json,
# which no longer exists now the site is adapter-free -- and because it was
# committed, it followed the repo to Cloudflare and failed every deploy.
.wrangler/

# dependencies
node_modules/

# logs
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# environment variables
.env
.env.production

# macOS-specific files
.DS_Store

# jetbrains setting folder
.idea/

# supabase CLI working files (config.toml and migrations ARE committed)
supabase/.branches/
supabase/.temp/

# Event images are pulled from Supabase Storage on every build; Storage is
# their source of truth, and deliverable 1b -- an object sync -- is what will
# back them up. Delete these two lines if you would rather have the flyers in
# git; note that data/snapshot.json no longer is, as of 2026-09-17.
src/assets/events/*
!src/assets/events/.gitkeep

# Snapshots are NOT committed. data/snapshot.json was until 2026-09-17, and
# this comment used to call it "the backup for content hand-entered over years,
# and what `--from-snapshot` publishes in an emergency".
#
# Measured the day it left the index: the committed copy was EIGHT DAYS STALE.
# fetched_at 2026-09-09, checksum ad26d21e...; production read 3642812f..., and
# a row's updated_at had moved on 14 September. fetch-content.mjs rewrites the
# file on every build, but only a human running a LOCAL build AND committing
# the result ever landed it in git -- CI rewrites it and commits nothing. So it
# updated when someone happened to, which since 9 September was never. A clone
# taken on 15 September would have republished 9 September's content silently.
#
# The backup is now a private repo, proven by a restore into dev and a diff.
# See supabase/ops/restore-content.sql and the rehearsal sequence.
#
# THE BARE NAME NEEDS ITS OWN LINE: `data/snapshot.*.json` does NOT match
# `data/snapshot.json` -- that glob wants a second dot. Without the first line
# below, the file returns as untracked after the next build and one `git add -A`
# commits it straight back. Which ref is production is one line in
# data/production-ref.
data/snapshot.json
data/snapshot.*.json
data/.snapshot-current

# public/_redirects is GENERATED by scripts/fetch-content.mjs from
# events.legacy_slugs, on every build including --from-snapshot. It was a
# tracked, hand-written file until 2026-09-19. The four rules it held are
# reproduced exactly from content, asserted byte-for-byte in
# tests/redirects.test.js BEFORE it stopped being tracked.
#
# Ignored rather than committed, for the reason the old one was dangerous: a
# committed copy only updates when a human runs a local build AND commits the
# result, which is exactly how data/snapshot.json went eight days stale. And a
# redirect that outlives the event it points at fails verify-build.mjs check 7
# and takes the whole deploy with it.
public/_redirects
Claude outputs/
